---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import lab from "../data/lab.json";
---
<Layout title="Methodology — Crawl Policy Index">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Contract</p>
    <h1>How a number is allowed to exist.</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      v1 measures <strong>robots.txt only</strong>. Policy that lives in HTTP 402 responses
      or signed-agent headers is out of scope and must not be inferred from these figures.
    </p>

    <h2>This preview is lab output</h2>
    <p>
      Agents in <code>registry/agents.yml</code> ship with <code>verified: false</code>.
      The published function <code>effective_policy(as_of_date)</code> therefore returns no rows.
      Figures here come from <code>{lab.view}</code> on panel <code>{lab.panel_version}</code>
      and must not be quoted as “the web.”
    </p>

    <h2>Panel</h2>
    <p>
      Every statistic is of a named, frozen panel. The current lab panel is
      <code>{lab.panel_version}</code> — the first 1,000 non-infrastructure pay-level domains
      from a pinned Tranco list. Re-running <code>panel/build_panel.py</code> with the same
      sources and seed must produce a byte-identical CSV.
    </p>

    <h2>Detect gate</h2>
    <p>
      Bodies at <code>/robots.txt</code> are classified before parsing. Rows classified
      <code>ambiguous</code>, <code>not_robots_html</code>, <code>not_robots_binary</code>,
      or <code>empty</code> are excluded from policy aggregates.
    </p>

    <h2>Effective policy</h2>
    <p><code>(domain, agent) → state</code> is resolved only by <code>effective_policy(as_of_date)</code>:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>verified and active agents only;</li>
      <li>an explicit interval when the file named the agent;</li>
      <li>otherwise the <code>*</code> group when one exists;</li>
      <li>otherwise <code>ALLOWED</code> / <code>NONE</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A domain with no trustworthy robots.txt is absent, not counted as allowing crawlers.
      Explicit and wildcard blocks are never conflated in a headline.
    </p>

    <h2>Tokens that are not crawlers</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Google-Extended</code>, <code>Applebot-Extended</code>, and other
      <code>opt_out_token</code> entries are control signals. They are never summed into
      crawler-blocking rates.
    </p>

    <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
    <p>
      Raw bytes are stored once per SHA-256 and never discarded. A parser bug is fixed by
      bumping <code>parse_version</code> and re-parsing. Prior parse rows are kept.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
